1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,200
Today, we will be discussing the dial-pulse map.

2
00:00:05,100 --> 00:00:17,000
On a rotary phone, you dialed each digit by turning
a wheel and letting it spin back.

3
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:29,500
As it spun back, it sent clicks down the line.
Dialing 2 sent two clicks.

4
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:41,500
Dialing 0 sent ten. The network called
those clicks dial pulses.

5
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:54,400
An area code is three digits, so its cost is three
numbers added together.

6
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:06,400
For New York's 212, that is 2 plus 1 plus 2.
Five pulses.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:21,900
South Dakota's 605 is 6 plus 10 plus 5. Twenty-one pulses,
more than four times as long to dial.

8
00:01:23,400 --> 00:01:39,300
In 1947 AT&T gave these 48 states 77 area codes.
Each one is colored by its pulses.

9
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:49,800
Gold codes were quick to dial.
Plum codes were slow.

10
00:01:51,300 --> 00:02:05,200
The quickest code of all, 212, went to New York.
It was the busiest phone market in the country.

11
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:19,700
Los Angeles got 213. Chicago got 312. Detroit got 313.
Six, six and seven pulses.

12
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:34,100
Did places with more people get quicker codes?
Each dot is one 1947 area code.

13
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:47,600
New York's 212 sits here: 5 pulses to dial,
and 7.9 million people living there in 1950.

14
00:02:48,200 --> 00:03:04,100
Codes of 5 to 9 pulses served a typical 2.7 million people.
Codes of 18 to 21 pulses served 0.8 million.

15
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:16,600
The pattern is loose. North Carolina's 704 took 21 pulses
and served 4.1 million people.

16
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:31,500
The middle digit carried a second message.
A 0 meant one code covered the whole state.

17
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:42,500
A 1 meant the state was divided
among several codes.

18
00:03:44,000 --> 00:04:02,900
As phones multiplied, the codes ran short. The first fix was
a split: cut a territory in two and give one part a new code.

19
00:04:03,500 --> 00:04:16,400
By 1991 the map held 117 codes,
all of them made by splitting.

20
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:36,400
In 1992 a second fix arrived, the overlay: a new code
laid over the same ground. Overlays are striped here.

21
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:50,400
By 2025 these 48 states carried 367 codes on 237 territories.
The last boundary split was in 2007.

22
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:55,600
That was the dial-pulse map. Thank you for watching.
